claims.text:
🟡 **Medium**

**agent_blocked is not cleared when a listing successfully re-confirms condition after a prior rewrite failure**

If a rewrite fails and exhausts retries, `onFailure` sets `agent_blocked=true` and reverts `status='condition_gate'`, `condition_confirmed=false`. When the user re-submits via the confirm-condition route and the rewrite succeeds this time, the final `transition-to-in-loop` step here only updates `status` and `condition_confirmed`-adjacent fields but does not clear `agent_blocked`/`agent_blocked_reason`. The listing will show as blocked in the dashboard/workspace even though the rewrite completed successfully — a stale error state that's confusing and requires manual intervention to clear, with no log or user-facing indication of why the listing still appears blocked.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
    await step.run('transition-to-in-loop', async () => {
      const supabase = getSupabaseAdmin()
      const { data: updated, error } = await supabase
        .from('listings')
        .update({ status: 'in_loop', agent_blocked: false, agent_blocked_reason: null })
        .eq('id', listingId)
        .eq('status', 'condition_gate')
        .select('id')
        .maybeSingle()
      if (error) {
        throw new Error(`description-rewrite: failed to advance listing ${listingId} to in_loop -- ${error.message}`)
      }
      if (!updated) {
        console.warn(`description-rewrite: zero rows updated advancing ${listingId} to in_loop — listing may no longer be in condition_gate (archived or concurrent transition)`)
      }
    })
```

replies.body:
Declining — the path is correct per the Inngest SDK type definition.

`FailureEventPayload` in `inngest/types.d.ts`:
```ts
type FailureEventPayload<P extends EventPayload = EventPayload> = {
  name: `${internalEvents.FunctionFailed}`;
  data: {
    function_id: string;
    run_id: string;
    error: ...;
    event: P;  // ← original event nested here
  };
};
```

In `onFailure({ event })`, `event` is the `FailureEventPayload`, not the original triggering event. The original event is at `event.data.event`, making `event.data.event.data.listingId` the correct path. The main function body uses `event.data.listingId` because there `event` IS the original `ListingConditionConfirmedEvent` — different handler, different event shape. Verified against `node_modules/inngest@4.18.1/types.d.ts`.
